# Logitech G915 TKL (046d:c343) on its cable, and through its LIGHTSPEED
# receiver (046d:c545), vendor HID++ interface -> uaccess for the seat user;
# and, on a kernel that gives it one, the keyboard's node of its own behind
# the receiver (408E, below), which carries what is typed on it too.
# The G913 TKL has the same ids. Not yet checked on this hardware: the ids
# and the interfaces are from its firmware image (MPK14.02_B0022), G HUB's
# device database and Solaar's records, read 2026-10-17.
#
# Interface 02, as on the G915. The keyboard's firmware image declares 00
# the boot keyboard, 01 a second keyboard collection and 02 the vendor
# collection (usage page 0xFF00, report ids 0x10 and 0x11), byte for byte the
# G915's. The c545's image holds its mouse, keyboard and vendor report
# descriptors in that order, which is the c541's measured layout; that 02 is
# the vendor one on the c545 is inferred from the order. gearbench-ctl report
# gives each node's interface and whether its report descriptor holds HID++,
# read from sysfs, so it needs no access to the node. Gearbench opens
# interface 02 alone on the cable and on the c545, so if HID++ is on another,
# put a copy of this file in /etc/udev/rules.d/ without the c343 line (the
# cable) or the c545 line (the receiver): it overrides this file by name, and
# a reinstall or upgrade leaves it alone. Then run sudo udevadm control
# --reload, replug the keyboard or the receiver, and paste the report's block
# into a device report. Read access to an interface that carries keystrokes
# would be a keylogger surface.
#
# Matched on properties, as 70-g915.rules is, for the reason it gives.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="046d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="c343", ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="02", TAG+="uaccess"

# The keyboard's node of its own behind the c545, on a kernel whose
# hid-logitech-dj binds the receiver (commit f70a82e20d01, merged for 7.3).
# Such a kernel gives the paired keyboard a hidraw node,
# 0003:046D:408E.<n>, as it gives the G502 X PLUS one behind its c547.
#
# This node carries the keyboard's keystrokes as well as its HID++ traffic
# (inferred from the c547, where the mouse's node carries what the mouse
# sends): a program that can read it reads what is typed on the G915 TKL. It
# carries no other device's while nothing else is paired to the receiver
# (70-g502x.rules says why). The owner granted it on 2026-10-18, through
# uaccess, as the G502 HERO's interface 01 was granted. udev may also give
# the node the receiver's properties, as it does on the c547, in which case
# the c545 line below grants it too.
#
# The grant crosses accounts, as 70-gearbench-uinput.rules says of
# /dev/uinput's: a program that opened the node keeps reading it after another
# account gets the seat, and so reads what that account types on the G915 TKL,
# a password at the login screen included. On a machine several people use,
# that file's group grant, in a copy of this file in /etc/udev/rules.d/,
# limits the node to the group's accounts.
#
# KERNELS matches a parent's kernel name, which for the paired device is
# bus:vendor:product.instance.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNELS=="0003:046D:408E.*", TAG+="uaccess"

# The receiver's own interface 02, where hid-generic holds the c545, as a
# 7.0.0 kernel does: the keyboard gets no node of its own, and its
# HID++ traffic comes over the receiver's vendor interface, as on the G915's
# c541. Its keystrokes come over the receiver's keyboard interface, 01 by
# the order above, which gets nothing.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="046d", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="c545", ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="02", TAG+="uaccess"
